In this lesson, we'll look at how to quickly compare and order numbers written in scientific notation, even when they're huge or tiny. Here's the key idea, and it's super simple. First, you only look at the exponents, those are the little numbers sitting on top of the ten. For positive numbers, the bigger the exponent, the bigger the whole number. So something times ten to the eight is way larger than something times ten to the three. Easy, right? But what if the exponents are the same? Then you just compare the coefficients, which are the numbers in front of the ten. The bigger coefficient means the bigger number. Now, when you're asked to order a whole list from smallest to largest, just sort by the exponent first, going from the most negative up to the most positive, and only worry about the coefficients when the exponents tie. That's honestly all there is to it. Now let's see it in action.